Package: pulsar
Architecture: all
# pipewire-bin, NOT pipewire. On Debian/Ubuntu the `pipewire` package ships
# only /usr/lib/systemd/user/pipewire.{service,socket}; the pw-dump, pw-top and
# pw-record binaries that this app actually execs live in `pipewire-bin`.
# Depending on `pipewire` happens to pull it in transitively but drags in
# systemd and sysusers too, and obscures what is genuinely required.
# Verified locally: `dpkg -S $(command -v pw-dump)` -> pipewire-bin.
# (Fedora names the same set `pipewire-utils`; see packaging/pulsar.spec.)
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 ${python3:Depends},
 python3 (>= 3.8),
 python3-tk,
 pulseaudio-utils,
 pipewire-bin,
Description: graphical audio routing manager for PulseAudio and PipeWire
 Pulsar is a Tkinter desktop application for routing audio between
 applications, sinks and sources on a PulseAudio or PipeWire server. It can
 list and inspect playback and capture streams, move an application to a
 different output exclusively (so its audio leaves the previous device rather
 than being duplicated there), create and connect sinks, and save routing
 setups as named presets and profiles.
 .
 Pulsar shells out to the audio server's own command line tools rather than
 linking against a client library: pactl from pulseaudio-utils for stream and
 sink control, and pw-dump, pw-top and pw-record from pipewire for server
 introspection and diagnostics. On Debian those are provided by the
 pulseaudio-utils and pipewire packages, which are therefore hard dependencies
 rather than suggestions.
 .
 python3-tk is a hard dependency because tkinter is imported at start-up, not
 lazily: without it the window never opens.
 .
 This package installs the Python module into the system dist-packages. That
 is safe only because the wheel ships exactly one top-level package, pulsar.
 Earlier versions also installed utils, ui and config as top-level modules,
 which collide with unrelated system packages in dist-packages.